## Objective
Learn how TCP Window Size controls the amount of data that can be transmitted before receiving an acknowledgment (ACK) and 
understand its importance in network performance analysis.


## What I Learned

- TCP Window Size controls how much data can be sent before waiting for an acknowledgment.
- A larger Window Size generally improves data transmission performance.
- A small Window Size causes the sender to wait for acknowledgments more frequently, reducing transmission speed.
- Window Size is an important TCP flow control mechanism.
- Security analysts analyze Window Size to troubleshoot slow or inefficient network communication.


